Cell["\<\
I'll show the development process interactively in class.  I may not able to \
explain all the features of the above program, but wen should be able to code \
a workable program similar to the above.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["The process of writing a program in Mathematica", "Title"],

Cell["First try, using straight lines", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For simplicity, we consider a version that use straight lines only.  Later \
on we change it to smooth curves.   We consider a set of horizontal lines and \
a set of vertical lines, and how these lines are get mapped by a complex \
function f.  Actually, if we use straight lines in the plot for the image \
lines, we consider the image points of intersection points in the grids only. \
The code below gives us only one horizontal line.  Note that a line passing \
through points {x1,y1}, {x2, y2}, {x3,y3} is represented in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as Line[{ {x1,y1}, {x2, y2}, ... }]."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(drawlines[{x0_, \ x1_, \ dx_}, \ {y0_, \ y1_, \ dy_}]\  := \ 
      Module[\ {}, \[IndentingNewLine]\ 
        y = \ y0; \[IndentingNewLine]oneline\  = \ 
          Table[\ {x, \ y}, {x, \ x0, \ x1, \ 
              dx}]; \[IndentingNewLine]oneline\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
I did not declare the local variables like oneline, x, y.  I am just lazy, \
but not a good practice, for in some cases, one can get unexpected results.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(drawlines[{0, \ 1, \ 0.2}, \ {0, \ 1, \ 0.2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{0, 0}, {0.2`, 0}, {0.4`, 0}, {0.6000000000000001`, 0}, {0.8`, 
        0}, {1.`, 0}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Show[Graphics[Line[%]]]\)], "Input"],

