Cell["\<\
Comparing to the version in the Graphics`ComplexMap` package, we still have a \
number of features missing.  For example, the increment dx, and dy can be \
omitted, if so, a globally define $Lines will be used to decided how many \
lines to draw.  In addition, the package version can add options in the \
form\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(CartesianMap[\(...\)\(,\)\(\ \)\(opt\  -> \ value\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
where opt and value are these allowed options for Plot or general graphics.   \
We'll not go into that details.  But show you the last feature: the package \
feature.   The main advantage of wrapping your code into a package is that \
the user can only access the public symbols and not the private symbols or \
definitions.   Also the package creates separate name space to void name \
conflict (between two set of programs).  Here is the stripped down outline of \
a package:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \( (*\ Summary : \ 
        This\ package\ is\ such\ and\ such\ a\ package\  ... \ *) \
\[IndentingNewLine]\(\(BeginPackage["\<Graphics`ComplexMap`\>"]\)\(\
\[IndentingNewLine]\)
    \)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(Unprotect[CartesianMap]\)\(\[IndentingNewLine]\)
    \)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(CartesianMap::usage\  = "\<CartesianMap[...] do such and such...\>"\
\)\(\[IndentingNewLine]\)
    \)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(Begin["\<`Private`\>"]\)\(\[IndentingNewLine]\)
    \)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\tCartesianMap[ ... ]\  := \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tModule[{}, \ \
\(\(...\)\(.\)\)]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\tPicture[ .. ]\  := \  ... \), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\t\(\(Curves[ .. ]\  := \  ... \)\(\[IndentingNewLine]\)
    \)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(End[]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Protect[CartesianMap]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(EndPackage[]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The above text (code) will be saved as a .m file, you load in the package \
with <<Graphics`ComplexMap`.   Then only the function Cartesian map is \
available to the user. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["SUMMARY OF MATHEMATICA", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
(1): Computation can be performed in user-specified precision.
(2): Ability to so symbolic computation with math formulas.
(3): Rich programming environment, support various programming styles.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Hope that you'll find Mathematica a very useful tool in your work.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Reading: Sec. 2.6.\n\nIf you have time, try to cover Part 1. A practical \
introduction, and Part 2. Principles, of the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " book."
}], "Title"]
},
